<!-- EXHAUST SYSTEM -->
<div class="panel" id="panel-exhaust">
<div class="wrap">
  <div class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--exhaust)">Diagram 4 · Exhaust System — Phase 23</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">Back Pressure Management · PCW Exhaust Valves · Steam Trap · Expansion Buffer · Condenser</h2>
  <p class="section-lead">
    Phase 23 adds a complete exhaust system for back pressure management. The locomotive blast pipe principle is applied in automated electronic form. Each piston has a dedicated PCW exhaust valve on its exhaust port. The PID controller times these valves to open at the optimal point in the piston stroke allowing exhaust steam to escape cleanly without fighting the next intake stroke. A steam trap downstream of the exhaust valves separates any condensate from the exhaust steam preventing water hammer. An expansion buffer chamber allows exhaust steam to expand and drop pressure before entering the condenser. This three component exhaust system replaces what a locomotive engineer managed manually by feel, reverser adjustment, and decades of experience — now handled automatically by the PID controller reading live pressure sensors.
    The Halloween steam whistle taps directly from the main steam supply line before the PCW inlet valves — it uses live boiler pressure and vents directly to atmosphere when the driver pulls the cord. No impact on the closed water loop.
  </p>

