  <h2 class="section-title" style="color:var(--next)">Phase 24 · Transmission Development · CVT Optimization · Uniflow Cylinders · RSR Variant</h2>
  <p class="section-lead">
    Phase 23 completes the steam generation and delivery system to a solid and buildable standard. Phase 24 focuses on the transmission and drivetrain optimization — specifically improving how the steam engine's power is transferred to the wheels more efficiently. Key areas for Phase 24 development are listed below. None of these require changes to the Aegis Boiler or water system which are now locked at Phase 23 specification.
  </p>

  <div class="next-box">
    <h4>◆ PHASE 24 PRIMARY FOCUS — TRANSMISSION AND DRIVETRAIN</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>CVT hydrostatic optimization: review the CRV-style CVT ratio range against actual steam engine torque and RPM curves — confirm the CVT is correctly specified for maximum efficiency at the 200 RPM steam engine output speed</li>
      <li>CVT hydraulic fluid cooler upgrade: Phase 21 noted the CVT cooler as mandatory — Phase 24 should properly size and specify the cooler based on actual heat rejection requirements at stall torque conditions which is the worst case thermal load</li>
      <li>Rear differential upgrade consideration: the current open rear diff for 2WD is adequate for Phase 23 but a limited slip differential in the rear axle would improve traction on wet or loose surfaces — straightforward swap same housing</li>
      <li>Front 2WD upgrade path: Phase 21 documentation noted "4WD option: add front prop in Phase 22" — Phase 24 is the right time to formally design the front drive option if desired — add front propshaft from CVT transfer output to front axle</li>
      <li>Amber driveshaft FEA: the full finite element analysis on the driveshaft at the highest load diff crossing was flagged as required in Phase 21 but not yet completed — Phase 24 must complete this before any road testing</li>
      <li>Transmission shift strategy programming: the PID controller currently manages boiler pressure and exhaust valve timing — Phase 24 should add CVT ratio control to the PID so the system automatically selects the optimal CVT ratio for current speed and load</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="next-box">
    <h4>◆ PHASE 24 SECONDARY FOCUS — RSR PERFORMANCE VARIANT PLANNING</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Uniflow cylinder design study: the Phase 23 exhaust PCW valve system is the simpler approach but uniflow cylinders with mid-bore exhaust ports eliminate back pressure more completely and at higher efficiency — Phase 24 should produce a design study comparing exhaust valve vs uniflow for the RSR variant</li>
      <li>Zoned rivet bowl design: the Phase 23 bowl uses uniform dome rivets throughout for simplicity — Phase 24 RSR variant should implement the predator mouth geometry: dome rivets at center for perpendicular impact, hybrid nipple rivets at mid-bowl, inward-angled cone rivets at rim for oblique capture — maximum flash conversion efficiency</li>
      <li>Twin Aegis Boiler study: at higher performance levels a single half-gallon boiler may limit steam output — Phase 24 should study twin boiler configuration feeding the two pistons independently — each piston has its own dedicated Aegis Boiler — eliminates cross-piston pressure interference</li>
      <li>Larger bore piston study: current 75mm bore is conservative — Phase 24 RSR study should examine 100mm or 120mm bore options — more swept volume per stroke means more torque and potentially higher speed from the same boiler pressure</li>
      <li>Higher operating pressure study: Aegis Boiler rated 400–500 PSI — Phase 24 should study operating at 150 or 200 PSI instead of 90 PSI — more than doubles available power from same piston size — requires recertification of all downstream components</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="next-box">
    <h4>◆ PHASE 24 TERTIARY FOCUS — REFINEMENTS AND TESTING</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>PID tuning protocol: Phase 24 should develop a formal PID tuning procedure for the combined inlet valve, exhaust valve, and EWI injection system — starting from cold and running through to operating temperature with logged data at each stage</li>
      <li>Cold start timing verification: the 15–20 second cold start estimate needs to be validated on the actual built system — Phase 24 should include a formal cold start test protocol with temperature and pressure logging at 1 second intervals</li>
      <li>Back pressure measurement: exhaust PSI sensors added in Phase 23 give live back pressure data — Phase 24 should log this data over a full operating cycle and use it to fine-tune PCW exhaust valve timing for minimum back pressure</li>
      <li>Filter service interval determination: run system on distilled water and on tap water emergency fill — measure filter differential pressure increase rate — set formal service intervals based on actual data rather than estimates</li>
      <li>Halloween whistle sound test: critical Phase 23 validation — must be tested at full 90 PSI boiler pressure — document the sound pressure level in decibels and ensure it is appropriately terrifying for road use</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

